king_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Reference the existing cross-process lock-contention idiom (tests/unit/test_write_lock_busy_probe_7084.py::test_probe_answers_a_lock_held_by_another_process — subprocess.Popen + stdout 'held' handshake) and reuse it as the model for the marker-lock contention test rather than inventing a new IPC harness.","cohort":["A1"],"criteria":["A1"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["tests/unit/test_write_lock_busy_probe_7084.py::test_probe_answers_a_lock_held_by_another_process already spawns a holder via subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, '-c', src, ...]) and synchronizes with 'proc.stdout.readline().strip() == \"held\"' — an explicit stdout handshake, no time.sleep as oracle.","That test is the same lock family (rebar._store.lock / lock_kernel), the same take_blocking_exclusive/fcntl path exercised by rebar.reducer.marker._marker_lock.","The plan text names no existing test/pattern to mirror, so the harness idiom is at risk of being reinvented rather than reused."],"finding":"The plan re-specifies a cross-process lock-contention test (two independent processes, explicit IPC sync, no time.sleep oracle) without referencing the existing, understood idiom already in the repo, risking a bespoke reinvention rather than reuse.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f352c655bca102147","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Investigation plan / Acceptance Criteria (bullets 3-4: 'two independent processes contending on the marker lock file' and 'explicit inter-process synchronization')","norm_id":"n148ae4cdafdc9382","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Author writes a new multiprocessing/subprocess harness from scratch and diverges from the repo's established synchronization convention, creating a second, slightly-different IPC idiom to maintain."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"In the Investigation plan, add a bullet directing the fix to mirror the subprocess.Popen + stdout-handshake pattern already used in test_write_lock_busy_probe_7084.py::test_probe_answers_a_lock_held_by_another_process, so the marker-lock test reuses the vetted process-contention/IPC-sync idiom.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.4286,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Have the RCA pin the exact failure mechanism (delay injected on the .archived open before flock acquisition, and remove_marker bypassing open via os.remove) and confirm it is truly the thread-vs-process distinction — not just the delay-injection site — before committing the two-process rewrite, so the new oracle synchronizes on actual lock acquisition/release rather than an incidental open().","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.2111,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'The current evidence points to a test oracle bug ... uses threading.Thread inside one Python process to test a POSIX flock critical section'","src/rebar/reducer/marker.py:24 `_marker_lock` opens the .lock file and calls take_blocking_exclusive on its own fd — distinct open descriptions contend under flock even in one process","src/rebar/_store/lock_kernel.py:166-168 POSIX leg is bare `fcntl.flock(fd, LOCK_EX)` (blocking, inode-mediated)","test line 60: delaying_open delays only when `os.fspath(path).endswith(ARCHIVE_MARKER_NAME)` — the marker file, opened only by write_marker (line 53); remove_marker uses os.remove (marker.py:77) and never triggers the delay"],"finding":"The plan's headline hypothesis (threaded oracle cannot serialize a process-level flock) is not clearly the true mechanism: POSIX flock(LOCK_EX) is per-open-file-description and kernel-mediated, so two threads opening distinct FDs on the same .lock file already contend within one process. The observed ordering failure more likely stems from WHERE the test injects its delay — delaying_open triggers only on the .archived marker path and blocks BEFORE take_blocking_exclusive runs, while remove_marker deletes via os.remove and never calls open(marker), so the second worker bypasses the delay entirely. The RCA step should pin this precise mechanism before committing to a two-process rewrite, or a rewritten oracle may not reproduce the RED / may mask the defect.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fa45a9e86326ea6a7","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Corrected scope boundary + Investigation plan (RCA gating)","norm_id":"nda4a92acdda15e91","priority":0.3889,"reason":"veto:cited-reference-inaccurate","recalled":false,"scenarios":["RCA concludes 'threads' is the cause and the two-process rewrite places its rendezvous at the same wrong point (marker-file open vs lock acquisition), so the new test passes without actually asserting lock-release-before-contender-enters","The real defect is delay placement; a process rewrite reproduces neither the old failure nor guards the true contract"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirm